# The assets conformance scene (docs/assets-plan.md, ratified
# 2026-08-18). `asset(name)` is a CORE call, and this scene is the only
# place its two halves are observed THROUGH A REAL SURFACE on every
# platform kaya ships.
#
# WHY AN IMAGE AND NOT `expect_typeface`. The typeface scene already
# proves the BLOB redemption — the vendored font's bytes go from the
# core's read straight into the platform's font API and never enter the
# guest — but iOS depth-stubs `expect_typeface`, which would have
# exempted the one lane whose asset delivery IS the shipped packaging
# mechanism (a copy into the bundle's Resources). An image needs no new
# verb, no interpreter arm and no wire constant: the guest hands the
# vendored mark's BYTES to an Image, the platform's own decoder decodes
# them, and the harness reads the decoded size off the real image view.
# That is the gallery scene's shape with an asset on the input side, and
# it is the BYTES redemption — the half of the ruling that nothing else
# in the tree observes at run time on any lane.
#
# THE CENSUS IS THE FORCING FUNCTION, and it is why the third line is so
# long. `asset_miss_sentence` answers the sentence a miss WOULD raise,
# totally, without unwinding — because unwinding is not one semantics in
# nine languages, and a Swift guest cannot catch its own fatalError at
# all. Line 1 of that sentence names what the package CARRIES, so this
# expectation can only pass on a lane that stages the WHOLE asset root
# rather than the one file it happens to need (docs/assets-plan.md A5.1,
# "the root moves as a unit"). Nothing else in the tree observes that
# property at run time; adding an asset reddens this line, and
# tools/check-assets.sh holds the frozen string equal to the root's own
# listing so the red arrives from a gate, naming this file, before any
# lane runs.
#
# THIS LINE ALSO PINS THE GRAMMAR. The sentence contains a `;`, which is
# the newline stand-in a transport without newlines uses (an Android
# intent extra). All three interpreters split statements on it and all
# three now do so QUOTE-AWARE — crates/kaya/src/harness.rs's
# `split_statements`, KayaSwiftUI.swift's and KayaCompose.kt's
# `kayaSplitStatements` — and this scene is the wall that holds them
# equal, because it runs the same quoted `;` through all three on five
# lanes.
#
# No opening settle: the first expect's bounded retry IS the scene-ready
# wait.
expect label#0 "assets"
expect image#0 "64x64"
expect label#1 "kaya: no asset named "icons/nope.png"; the package carries fonts/OFL.txt, fonts/README.md, fonts/sora-wght.ttf, icons/README.md, icons/kaya-mark.png, identity.toml, win/README.md, win/minimal-resources.pri"
# The other end of the same query, and the large asset. 111400 is the
# vendored font's whole size (guests/assets/fonts/README.md says why it
# ships whole), so a reader that truncated into a fixed buffer fails
# here rather than passing with a sentence that still looked right. The
# tail is the query's POSITIVE answer: for a name that resolves it
# returns the empty string, and "no complaint" is this scene's rendering
# of that — when it is not empty the guest shows the sentence instead,
# so a failure prints what was measured.
expect label#2 "fonts/sora-wght.ttf: 111400 bytes, no complaint"
